{"visualPremise":"Flat 2D stickman explainer showing the contrast between an imagined, theatrical Viking (horned) and the sparse archaeological reality (plain iron helmets and ritual Bronze Age objects), switching between battlefield dramatization, museum close-ups, and 19th-century stage reconstructions.","visualTone":"matter-of-fact and slightly wry: clear, evidence-first visuals that puncture grand myth with calm, almost deadpan comparisons; drama when showing the myth (stage lighting, exaggerated horns), restrained and neutral when showing archaeological reality (muted palettes, clinical framing).","cast":[{"id":"viewer_viking","role":"the viewer as a Viking-era raider (second-person avatar for 'you're charging' moments)"},{"id":"viewer_modern","role":"the viewer in modern-day scenes (museum visitor, seeing postcards, modern consumer)"},{"id":"viking_warrior_archetype","role":"generic Viking warrior used whenever the narration shows the mythic or the archaeological typical warrior"},{"id":"bronze_age_priest_archetype","role":"representative Bronze Age ritual figure used when showing the Veksø and other ceremonial helmets"},{"id":"opera_costume_designer_archetype","role":"19th-century theatrical costumer or stage figure used to show how horned imagery was popularized on stage"},{"id":"archaeologist_archetype","role":"modern archaeologist or museum curator who explains finds"}],"settings":[{"id":"S1","label":"Viking-era battlefield/hillside dramatization"},{"id":"S2","label":"Bronze Age ritual site / bog/hoard setting"},{"id":"S3","label":"19th-century theater backstage / stage (Wagnerian production)"},{"id":"S4","label":"Museum/gallery close-up display and modern postcard/mascot contexts"},{"id":"S5","label":"modern city/stadium (for mascot/football logo shots)"}],"props":[{"id":"gjermundbu_helmet","label":"Gjermundbu helmet (canonical illustration): plain rounded iron cap with a slightly flattened dome, dark iron flat-fill, and a prominent vertical nasal guard extending down the face's center; visible rivet heads at the rim only where the cap sections meet, no sockets or holes for horns, no cheek plates attached. Presented on a neutral museum pedestal with a small rectangular placard reading 'Gjermundbu helmet, c. 970 CE'."},{"id":"vekso_helmet","label":"Veksø bronze horned helmet (canonical illustration): very thin decorative bronze cap with two long gracefully curving horns rising symmetrically from the sides and tipped with small flat circular discs; bronze rendered as flat warm-gold color with thin black outline; overall delicate silhouette suggesting ceremonial use, not combat. Displayed on a low plinth with a placard reading 'Veksø helmets, Nordic Bronze Age, c. 1000 BCE'."},{"id":"wagner_theatrical_horn_helmet","label":"Wagnerian theatrical horned helmet (canonical illustration): oversized stylized helmet with exaggerated glossy-gold curved horns (thicker base, sweeping upward), heavy theatrical fur trim around the base, and visible costume fastenings; designed for stage spectacle rather than practicality. Presented in a theater backstage rack or shown worn by the opera_costume_designer_archetype in a costume sketch."},{"id":"museum_postcard_and_mascot","label":"Museum postcard/mascot depiction (canonical illustration): flattened, high-contrast graphic of a horned Viking silhouette in bright saturated colors (e.g., crimson background, black silhouette with gold horns) framed like a postcard. For mascot: a rounded-corner stylized horned helmet logo with two oversized horns colored bright gold on a team-blue background. Include a small tag-line or label when used ('Souvenir postcard' or 'Vikings (mascot)')."},{"id":"excavation_tray_and_trowel","label":"Excavation tray and trowel (canonical illustration): small rectangular artifact tray with soft padding and a single labeled artifact tag reading 'fragment' next to a tiny rusted iron fragment; trowel is a small flat triangular silhouette with a wooden handle. Colors: tray light gray, trowel handle brown, tool metal dark gray."}],"graphicLanguage":{"comparisonLayouts":"Two-column horizontal splits for direct comparisons: left column = myth/theatrical imagery (saturated), right column = archaeological reality (muted). Keep objects centered on each column with matching scale for immediate visual comparison.","timelineConventions":"Simple horizontal timeline with three labeled nodes: 'Nordic Bronze Age (c.1000 BCE)' at left with a small bronze helmet icon; 'Viking Age (c.793–1066 CE)' in center with Gjermundbu helmet icon; '19th century / modern' at right with a stage/mascot icon. Use muted baseline color and node accents in bronze-gold for the Bronze Age node and dull-iron-gray for the Viking node.","symbolicMetaphorLanguage":"Use theatrical spotlight and stage curtain as recurring metaphors for 'myth as performance' and museum pedestals/placards as metaphors for 'evidence and labeling'."},"factualVisualConstraints":["Do not invent rivet holes or attachment points on Gjermundbu or other Viking-age iron helmets that would imply horns were attached.","Show Veksø helmets as Bronze Age objects dated centuries before the Viking Age, never presented as contemporaneous Viking military equipment.","When showing archaeological excavation scenes, suggest that organic materials (leather, antler) may have decayed by showing empty soil with labeled 'no surviving organic attachment' rather than fabricating preserved leather straps."]}

CALLBACK PLAN: planted detail "two long curved horns" — plant in segment seg1, payoff in segment seg14.

